WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:05.101
Written and directed by Leigh Whannell, The
Invisible Man is a rock-solid thriller, but

00:00:05.101 --> 00:00:07.890
some of its mysteries are never fully resolved.

00:00:07.890 --> 00:00:12.440
However, we'll be breaking down all of the
questions that the film never gave us answers

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.840
to, to see if we can answer them ourselves.

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:20.080
Pretty much every scene in The Invisible Man
is about Cecilia struggling, literally or

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:23.970
metaphorically, with Adrian's lingering presence
in her life.

00:00:23.970 --> 00:00:28.770
The film's singular focus on this one plotline
is generally a good thing, since it offers

00:00:28.770 --> 00:00:32.560
the audience no escape from the constantly
escalating tension.

00:00:32.560 --> 00:00:37.600
But unfortunately, it also means that there's
basically no room in the film for subplots,

00:00:37.620 --> 00:00:42.540
so we don't get a chance to learn much about
who Cecilia is as a person, outside of her

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:44.560
relationship with Adrian.

00:00:44.560 --> 00:00:50.160
In the brief moments where Cecilia is momentarily
free of Adrian's grasp, we catch glimpses

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:53.720
of a truly compelling character who we want
to learn more about.

00:00:53.720 --> 00:00:58.590
When she, James, and Sydney are just hanging
out together, Cecilia is sharp, funny, and

00:00:58.590 --> 00:00:59.590
empathetic.

00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:05.080
Also, in one scene, Cecilia is suddenly being
interviewed for a job at an architecture firm,

00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:09.159
the only acknowledgement we get of Cecilia
having career aspirations.

00:01:09.159 --> 00:01:10.579
Was she an architect before?

00:01:10.579 --> 00:01:12.840
Did she give it up for Adrian?

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.880
One reason why Cecilia sometimes feels like
such a cipher is because even she herself

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:20.640
might not even know who she is anymore.

00:01:20.640 --> 00:01:25.799
Trauma can make you feel like whoever you
once were, that person has now been erased.

00:01:25.799 --> 00:01:29.149
You have no life outside your struggle for
survival.

00:01:29.149 --> 00:01:31.659
Who was Cecilia before she met Adrian?

00:01:31.659 --> 00:01:36.520
The film doesn't answer, and we'd really like
to know, but perhaps Cecilia is currently

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:39.280
in a place where not even she remembers.

00:01:39.800 --> 00:01:44.799
From the moment Emily rescues Cecilia from
Adrian's house, something seems off about

00:01:44.800 --> 00:01:46.320
their interactions.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.880
Emily is never seen comforting her sister,
most of their conversations quickly turn to

00:01:51.880 --> 00:01:56.979
bickering, and most tragically of all, when
strange things start happening to Cecilia,

00:01:56.979 --> 00:02:00.200
Emily doesn't take her sister's concerns seriously.

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:05.119
One possible interpretation of their dynamic
is that it's solely a necessary plot device

00:02:05.120 --> 00:02:08.080
in order for the horror of the film to function.

00:02:08.080 --> 00:02:13.000
As is the case with many horror stories, the
scares can only continue if no one listens

00:02:13.000 --> 00:02:14.930
to the protagonist.

00:02:14.930 --> 00:02:18.100
But there's also a more charitable reading
of their dynamic.

00:02:18.100 --> 00:02:23.240
Long before Cecilia escaped from Adrian, she
and Emily were already estranged.

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:27.660
Think about it, the way that Cecilia's stalker
drives a wedge between her and Emily is by

00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:33.200
sending a single fake email, telling Emily
that Cecilia wishes she was dead.

00:02:33.200 --> 00:02:34.840
What happens next?

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:38.280
Emily immediately cuts Cecilia out of her
life.

00:02:38.280 --> 00:02:42.840
Cecilia denies that she sent the email, but
Emily refuses to listen.

00:02:42.860 --> 00:02:47.770
If that email was a wildly out of character
thing for Cecilia to say, you'd think that

00:02:47.770 --> 00:02:52.020
Emily would consider the possibility that
she didn't have the full picture, and she'd

00:02:52.020 --> 00:02:54.200
listen to Cecilia's explanation.

00:02:54.200 --> 00:02:58.680
Emily's reaction only makes sense if there
was already some underlying tension between

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:02.060
the sisters that Cecilia's tormentor was exploiting.

00:03:02.060 --> 00:03:07.460
We never get the full story about the reason
for this tension, but some old wound is undeniably

00:03:07.460 --> 00:03:08.440
there.

00:03:08.840 --> 00:03:13.920
After Cecilia is arrested for her sister's
murder, a nurse meets with her and tells her

00:03:13.930 --> 00:03:17.500
that, according to a blood test, Cecilia is
pregnant.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:18.500
The nurse says,

00:03:18.500 --> 00:03:21.520
"The test shows us that it was pretty recent.

00:03:21.520 --> 00:03:28.060
We can't be sure of exactly when, but it must
have been some time in the last month."

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:33.100
So now comes an unpleasant question...when
and how did Cecilia get pregnant?

00:03:33.100 --> 00:03:38.740
At this point, we don't know exactly how long
it's been since Cecilia escaped from Adrian.

00:03:38.740 --> 00:03:43.740
Two weeks passed between when she first left
and when she heard of his apparent suicide.

00:03:43.740 --> 00:03:48.220
Since then, we're guessing that at least a
few more weeks have probably elapsed, if not

00:03:48.220 --> 00:03:51.630
significantly longer, perhaps even a couple
months.

00:03:51.630 --> 00:03:55.760
It's possible, though a bit of a stretch,
that Adrian got Cecilia pregnant while they

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:59.680
were still living together, but it's also
possible that, and here's where things get

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:03.650
real uncomfortable, he did so after she'd
already left.

00:04:03.650 --> 00:04:06.930
We don't want to think about the mechanics
of how exactly that would work, but given

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:12.061
what we know about Adrian, both in terms of
his immorality and his resourcefulness, it's

00:04:12.061 --> 00:04:13.630
not out of the question.

00:04:13.630 --> 00:04:17.449
One other lingering question that the film
doesn't answer is what's going to happen to

00:04:17.449 --> 00:04:19.719
Cecilia's unwanted pregnancy?

00:04:19.719 --> 00:04:24.169
We can understand why the film didn't go there,
but whether or not Cecilia plans on keeping

00:04:24.169 --> 00:04:28.760
it is perhaps one of the biggest questions
that the film chooses to leave unanswered.

00:04:28.760 --> 00:04:33.400
When Cecilia finally shoots the invisible
assailant in James' house and peels the suit

00:04:33.400 --> 00:04:39.039
off him, she's surprised to find not Adrian
inside but rather his simpering toad of a

00:04:39.039 --> 00:04:40.039
brother, Tom.

00:04:40.040 --> 00:04:42.480
There are a few different ways to interpret
this reveal.

00:04:42.480 --> 00:04:47.640
One is that Tom really was the one tormenting
Cecilia all this time, and Adrian never got

00:04:47.640 --> 00:04:49.080
his hands dirty personally.

00:04:49.080 --> 00:04:54.240
Another interpretation is that this is the
only time Tom wears the suit, perhaps because

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:59.120
Adrian is attempting to frame him, but at
all other points, Adrian is the only one abusing

00:04:59.120 --> 00:04:59.760
her.

00:04:59.760 --> 00:05:04.349
Either of these explanations are potentially
defensible, but there's another interesting

00:05:04.349 --> 00:05:05.349
option.

00:05:05.349 --> 00:05:10.110
It's also possible that, during the final
invasion of James' house (and perhaps earlier),

00:05:10.110 --> 00:05:15.360
there are two invisible men, Tom and Adrian,
working together simultaneously.

00:05:15.360 --> 00:05:19.611
After all, when Sydney finally confronts the
invisible man in her room by macing him, he

00:05:19.611 --> 00:05:25.009
collapses to the ground, but then, a moment
later, when Sydney runs out into the hallway,

00:05:25.009 --> 00:05:26.770
she's immediately knocked over.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:31.180
It's possible that it's the same guy, but
the logistics of the scene make way more sense

00:05:31.180 --> 00:05:35.099
if there was actually a second invisible man
waiting in the hallway.

00:05:35.099 --> 00:05:38.219
We know that Adrian has access to multiple
suits.

00:05:38.219 --> 00:05:42.800
And don't forget, when Adrian is discovered
by police the next day, his eyes look awfully

00:05:42.800 --> 00:05:43.760
red.

00:05:43.760 --> 00:05:45.880
Perhaps like someone who was recently maced?

00:05:47.180 --> 00:05:51.569
Throughout much of the film, Adrian and his
brother, Tom, appear to be in a more or less

00:05:51.569 --> 00:05:55.169
equal partnership, a unified front of awfulness.

00:05:55.169 --> 00:05:59.229
But occasionally, we get hints that their
relationship might be a lot more complicated.

00:05:59.229 --> 00:06:04.240
At one point, Tom asserts that Adrian held
all the power in their relationship, saying,

00:06:04.240 --> 00:06:08.819
"My brother controlled me long before he met
you, Cecilia."

00:06:08.819 --> 00:06:13.800
Later, after Tom is killed, Adrian claims
that the complete opposite is true, saying,

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:22.720
"I know it didn't seem like it to the outside
world, but Tom controlled me."

00:06:22.729 --> 00:06:27.770
If both brothers claim that they were the
abused and powerless party in a toxic relationship,

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:28.969
who should we believe?

00:06:28.969 --> 00:06:33.599
We feel the evidence points towards Tom being
subservient to Adrian and not the other way

00:06:33.599 --> 00:06:34.599
around.

00:06:34.599 --> 00:06:38.259
When Tom visits Cecilia in the psych ward,
he tells her that they can get her out of

00:06:38.259 --> 00:06:41.729
jail if she agrees to return to her life with
Adrian.

00:06:41.729 --> 00:06:46.500
This ultimatum doesn't really make sense if
Tom has the power and Adrian is just a puppet.

00:06:46.500 --> 00:06:50.509
That being said, it's also potentially true
that both brothers are telling the truth,

00:06:50.509 --> 00:06:51.589
at least in part.

00:06:51.589 --> 00:06:54.619
Maybe they each abused one another at various
times.

00:06:54.619 --> 00:06:59.229
Or perhaps both are lying, and their claims
of abuse exist solely to manipulate Cecilia's

00:06:59.229 --> 00:07:00.229
emotions.

00:07:00.229 --> 00:07:04.499
Regardless, none of this justifies the way
that either of them behaves, so it also seems

00:07:04.499 --> 00:07:08.320
safe to just label them both as irredeemable
jerks and move on with our lives.

00:07:09.440 --> 00:07:13.749
Since there's still arguably a bit of ambiguity
at the end of the film regarding just how

00:07:13.749 --> 00:07:19.439
bad of a guy Adrian is, is Cecilia's decision
to murder him truly justifiable?

00:07:19.439 --> 00:07:24.340
During Adrian and Cecilia's final dinner conversation,
Cecilia is wearing a wire to record their

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:25.340
conversation.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:29.809
Presumably, she's doing this to attempt to
trick Adrian into confessing his crimes on

00:07:29.809 --> 00:07:32.210
tape, so that she can send him to jail.

00:07:32.210 --> 00:07:36.789
Adrian, however, is evasive, and he starts
trying to seduce and gaslight Cecilia all

00:07:36.789 --> 00:07:37.949
over again.

00:07:37.949 --> 00:07:39.979
This culminates with him using the phrase,

00:07:39.979 --> 00:07:45.680
"That shouldn't come as a surprise."

00:07:45.680 --> 00:07:51.539
As Adrian says these words, a twinge of realization
flashes across Cecilia's face, presumably

00:07:51.539 --> 00:07:54.539
because this has just confirmed two things
for her.

00:07:54.539 --> 00:07:59.979
The first comes from how he says that word,
"surprise," in his deep, raspy voice.

00:07:59.979 --> 00:08:05.129
That same word was used mockingly by Cecilia's
invisible stalker as he was tormenting her,

00:08:05.129 --> 00:08:09.809
and hearing that word again in Adrian's voice
presumably confirms for her that they are

00:08:09.809 --> 00:08:11.030
the same person.

00:08:11.030 --> 00:08:15.560
We can also assume that this confirms for
Cecilia that Adrian is still willing to lie

00:08:15.560 --> 00:08:19.040
to her, willing to start up the cycle of abuse
all over again.

00:08:19.040 --> 00:08:23.920
Adrian truly has no remorse, no conscience,
and he's never going to stop.

00:08:23.920 --> 00:08:27.440
That was enough for Cecilia to conclude that
Adrian needed to die.

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:29.480
Does that make what she did right?

00:08:29.480 --> 00:08:34.760
In the end, Cecilia decides to kill Adrian
by putting on an invisibility suit and forcing

00:08:34.780 --> 00:08:39.740
him to cut his own throat with a knife, in
full view of the house's security cameras,

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:42.280
so that he appears to be committing suicide.

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:46.840
It's a shocking and gut-wrenching moment,
but it also has the potential to be somewhat

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:47.840
confusing.

00:08:47.840 --> 00:08:53.270
After all, the exact logistics of how Cecilia
accomplished this plan aren't ever fully explained.

00:08:53.270 --> 00:08:58.200
When and how exactly did she get her hands
on an invisibility suit of her own?

00:08:58.200 --> 00:09:02.620
One option is that Cecilia is wearing Tom's
suit from when he attacked James' house.

00:09:02.620 --> 00:09:05.440
However, this seems unlikely for two reasons.

00:09:05.440 --> 00:09:09.650
First, the suit was clearly damaged after
Cecilia shot it to pieces.

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:14.950
The second problem is that Tom's suit was
presumably turned over to the police as evidence.

00:09:14.950 --> 00:09:19.970
What seems to be a far more likely option
is that Cecilia's suit is actually the one

00:09:19.970 --> 00:09:24.800
that she discovered earlier in the basement
when she crept back into Adrian's house halfway

00:09:24.800 --> 00:09:25.890
through the film.

00:09:25.890 --> 00:09:30.440
In this scene, she peels it off a mannequin,
presumably to steal it, but when she hears

00:09:30.440 --> 00:09:35.780
someone entering the house, Cecilia appears
to stash the suit away in an air vent inside

00:09:35.780 --> 00:09:37.000
Adrian's closet.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:41.690
Later, when Cecilia claims that she's going
to the bathroom during her dinner with Adrian,

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:46.000
she's most likely retrieving the suit from
the closet and using it to execute her master

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:46.920
plan.

00:09:46.920 --> 00:09:51.960
After Cecilia's final confrontation with Adrian,
the film ends rather abruptly.

00:09:51.960 --> 00:09:56.311
Even though, in this moment, Cecilia seems
to have found some measure of closure, we

00:09:56.311 --> 00:09:59.940
still have endless questions about what comes
next for her.

00:09:59.940 --> 00:10:03.570
First, is Cecilia going to jail for any of
the stuff she's done?

00:10:03.570 --> 00:10:08.000
She may not have actually committed her sister's
murder, but she did break out of a mental

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:11.310
hospital, and she did kill both Tom and Adrian.

00:10:11.310 --> 00:10:14.160
However, we think she's going to be just fine.

00:10:14.160 --> 00:10:19.050
Numerous people "saw" an invisible suspect
menacing Cecilia in the hospital, which means

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:21.600
shooting Tom was legitimately self-defense.

00:10:21.600 --> 00:10:24.990
Plus, her plan for eliminating Adrian was
rock solid.

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:31.320
And if things go bad, Cecilia also just inherited
$5 million, so she can probably afford some

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:32.320
pretty good lawyers.

00:10:33.160 --> 00:10:37.640
One of the reasons that The Invisible Man
has been so critically well-received is because

00:10:37.650 --> 00:10:41.350
it succeeds on numerous different levels simultaneously.

00:10:41.350 --> 00:10:43.550
It's not just a vehicle for scares.

00:10:43.550 --> 00:10:48.320
It's also a film with a brain, full of deeper
themes about trauma and abuse.

00:10:48.320 --> 00:10:51.730
But what exactly is it trying to say about
these subjects?

00:10:51.730 --> 00:10:56.200
First, in regards to trauma, the film shows
us that even when the source of your pain

00:10:56.200 --> 00:11:00.250
is gone, trauma stays with you like an invisible
monster.

00:11:00.250 --> 00:11:05.050
We see that this is the case for Cecilia even
before the invisible man comes back into her

00:11:05.050 --> 00:11:06.160
new life.

00:11:06.160 --> 00:11:11.140
She's afraid to leave the house, she has trouble
sleeping, and she jumps at shadows.

00:11:11.140 --> 00:11:16.270
Metaphorically, Cecilia's tormentor is a personification
of trauma, an invisible force that stands

00:11:16.270 --> 00:11:20.440
at the foot of her bed at night, follows her
down the street, and pulls her away from her

00:11:20.440 --> 00:11:21.680
friends and family.

00:11:21.680 --> 00:11:26.140
And second, in regards to abuse, The Invisible
Man shows us that when people come forward

00:11:26.140 --> 00:11:30.000
and claim that they're being abused, we tend
not to believe them.

00:11:30.000 --> 00:11:34.470
We downplay their stories, invalidate their
feelings, and call them "crazy."

00:11:34.470 --> 00:11:38.850
Pretty much everyone in the film does this
to Cecilia at some point, even her sister

00:11:38.850 --> 00:11:39.900
and her friend.

00:11:39.900 --> 00:11:44.560
This film is a reminder that just because
we can't see the people around us being abused,

00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:46.320
that doesn't mean it isn't happening.

